 - Fix K-Mod some allocation pathologies: e.g. starving/stagnant cities no longer choose **+1 hammer** over **+4 food** unworked plots; also fix the "food is production" valuation so settlers/workers don’t undervalue food. See [KI#34](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#34---seemingly-fixedtweaked-major-k-mod-suboptimal-food-plot-ai-city-allocation-starving-or-stagnant-cities-prefer-1-hammer-over-4-food-plots-that-are-unallocated-to-any-population-ulundi-screenshots-prague-screenshots-and-others--food-not-being-valued-when-food-is-production-eg-worker-settler-etc-if-any-more-when-it-is-in-fact-the-exact-same-and-probably-more-reliable-to-set-rather-than-production).
 - If happiness room is **high**, push growth harder: favor food tiles more, deemphasize hammers (unless **food is currently production**). See [KI#40](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#40---seemingly-fixed--addressed-tremendousmajor-plot-allocation-improvement-2-if-we-are-high-in-happiness-favour-food-tiles-much-more-and-hammer-tiles-much-less-if-happiness-is-really-high-favour-growth-dynamically-and-plot-allocation-of-these-the-more-we-can-afford-it-even-if-production-is-slightly-reduced-short-term-as-a-result-it-auto-corrects-at-high-happiness-with-seemingly-much-stronger-ai-and-developped-cities-as-a-result-excluded-if-food-is-production-eg-worker-settler-etc-if-i-am-not-mistaken-but).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5927+) Update: now mitigated/halted by **high food loss from unhealthiness**, where it is inefficient to grow more (e.g., York size 25 with 25 health 36 unhealth (-11 food) and 39 happy 27 unhappy, +3 food per turn still, should not grow more and work specialists or other yields than food rather to increase efficiency).
-- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5989+) **Recheck and reassign citizens to safe, clearly better unworked plots:** K-Mod could keep a weaker worked (i.e. assigned to one population in city) plot because its quick initial estimate overlooked another plot that its full city-aware comparison considered better. If ordinary citizen allocation finds no change, the **AI now rechecks plots that improve at least one yield without reducing any**, **trade some production for a large food/commerce gain**, or otherwise **offer a substantial overall gain without losing food**. It switches **only when** the full city-aware valuation confirms the change is **beneficial**, never when the swap would cause starvation, and does not immediately reverse a plot assigned earlier in the same update. The newly added `SAS_BBAI_CITIZEN_LOG_LEVEL` BBAI logging test showed this corrected cases such as Karakorum working **2 food / 0 hammers / 1 commerce** instead of the strictly better **2 food / 1 hammer / 1 commerce** plot, made 624 approved swaps through turn 359 at normal game speed, and reduced remaining plot-to-plot contextual misses from 4,187 to 3,229 (about 23%); specialist-related swaps remain a separate target.
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5989+) **Recheck and reassign citizens to safe, clearly better unworked plots:** K-Mod could keep a weaker worked (i.e. assigned to one population in city) plot because its quick initial estimate overlooked another plot that its full city-aware comparison considered better. If ordinary citizen allocation finds no change, the **AI now rechecks plots that improve at least one yield without reducing any**, **trade some production for a large food/commerce gain**, or otherwise **offer a substantial overall gain without losing food**. It switches **only when** the full city-aware valuation confirms the change is **beneficial**, never when the swap would cause starvation, and does not immediately reverse a plot assigned earlier in the same update. The newly added `SAS_BBAI_CITIZEN_LOG_LEVEL` BBAI logging test showed this corrected cases such as Karakorum working **2 food / 0 hammers / 1 commerce** instead of the strictly better **2 food / 1 hammer / 1 commerce** plot, made 624 approved swaps through turn 359 at normal game speed, and reduced remaining plot-to-plot contextual misses from 4,187 to 3,229 (about 23%); specialist-related swaps remain a separate target. (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5990+) Note: for convenience, human cities use the same fallback by default (tunable) through `SAS_CONVENIENCE_HUMAN_CITIZEN_PLOT_FALLBACK_ENABLE`, but only while citizen automation is enabled; manual citizen assignments remain untouched.
 
 #### City Production (AI)
 
@@ -205,6 +205,7 @@ Note: cultural victory bullets as of now assume 3 legendary cities are required,
 - Never pick **Citizen** specialist (very inefficient). Implemented in `CvCityAI::AI_jobChangeValue`. See [KI#44.5](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#445---disallow-citizen-specialist-as-it-is-a-really-bad-choice-unless-absolutely-necessary).
 - Add **sanity guards**: in small cities (and larger ones with headroom), don’t newly assign specialists until growth caps are reached (with food/happiness checks). **Barbarian** cities, on top of these rules, may only run **Scientist** specialists when they must run any. See [KI#45](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#45---addressed--patched--worked-around-ai-cities-assigning-too-soon-orand-too-often-specialists-resulting-in-early-stagnation-very-inefficiently-now-added-sanity-rules-to-not-go-for-a-specialist).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5987+) Update: existing specialists **can still switch to a better specialist type** because doing so **does not reduce worked population or growth**. BBAI culture diagnostics exposed this when Barcelona had 8 existing Priests/Merchants but the growth guard forbade considering any switch between specialist types; normal valuation can now compare them and choose another type (e.g. Artist, Engineer, Scientist) when better.
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5990+) **Reassign existing AI specialists to a better plot or specialist type:** K-Mod's quick initial estimate could leave a city population member assigned as a specialist even when K-Mod's existing full city-aware comparison rated an available plot or another specialist type higher. The AI now applies that full comparison through an additional pass that can reassign the specialist to the better plot or specialist type (an assignable free-specialist slot (counted by `totalFreeSpecialists()`), which does not use a population member, may change to a better allowed type; fixed-type free specialists (`getFreeSpecialistCount(type)`) remain unchanged, and explicitly AI/DLL-forced specialist assignments are not changed by this pass). This pass only reassigns existing specialists; it never removes a population member from a worked city plot to create a new specialist, preserving the AdvCiv-SAS growth-first safeguards against excessive specialist assignment, and every change must still pass the full city-aware valuation and starvation checks. Compared with the preceding BBAI run without this additional reassignment pass, both measured through turn 402, the new run made 1,963 approved reassignments (918 to plots and 1,045 between specialist types) and reduced remaining logged specialist-origin misses from 2,601 to 0. As a directional sanity check within the new run rather than a before/after comparison, this pass made 201 reassignments from Artist to another job and 46 reassignments to Artist.
 - Exception to the sanity guards rule, and AI enhancement: if a city hasn’t expanded to its **BFC** yet, auto-assign exactly **one Artist** to pop borders, then stop once secured. Implemented in `CvCity::AI_doTurn`.
 
